   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.1. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement a new generation of gender-responsive national employment policies, including for youth</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Ministry of Employment and Labour Relations (MELR) of Uzbekistan submitted to the Cabinet the national employment strategy 2021-2030. Youth employment promotion policies are mainstreamed in the strategy, with a focus on facilitating youth transition from school to work and measures to address future of work challenges, namely improving the quality of active labour market programs, developing a TVET system in accordance the requirements of the labour market, prompting employment of vulnerable group (women and people with disabilities) and ensuring effective mobility withing the country and supporting labour migrants.

Furthermore, in 2020, the formation of a &quot;youth booklet&quot; has begun in order to systematize the work with unemployed youth and young people in need of social protection; in 2021, the country launched a new system of working with youth, one of the main areas of which is to support youth entrepreneurship, increase youth employability through vocational training and establishing a national network of youth employment and career guidance centres. The ILO supported the preparatory work for the new national employment policies emphasizing youth and women. In February 2020, the ILO submitted to tripartite discussion a first report analyzing the institutional and regulatory framework for employment policies (&quot;Background report for the preparation of the National Employment Strategy of the Republic of Uzbekistan&quot;). Subsequently, complementing this report, the ILO prepared an in-depth diagnosis of the labour market and proposing a set of strategic employment objectives, &quot;(Towards Full and Productive Employment in Uzbekistan: Achievements and Challenges&quot; in May 2020); the report was updated in 2021, and expanded including new sections concerning to the COVID 19 job crisis and the youth labour market. These country reports were submitted for and validated in tripartite discussions organized in 2020. In April 2021, the ILO organized a subregional country case webinar where the latter report on Uzbekistan was presented and submitted to peer-to-peer discussions in the CIS network for youth employment policies.

The ILO reports were presented in several meeting with constituents, stakeholders, members of the parliament and the UN community; among them, the Round-table on setting up priorities and outcomes of a new DWCP Uzbekistan 2021-2025 (January 2021); and the conference Uzbekistan progress towards SDG 8: employment, productivity and poverty reduction (June 2021).

Complementing the preparation of the employment strategy, the ILO has provided support for improving the national capacity to measure remote work; and the capacity of budgeting employment policies.

These results have been achieved within the context of the public-private partnership between the ILO and the Russian company LUKOIL, which provided financial support to the implementation of the project &quot;Strategic partnerships for youth employment in the CIS&quot;.</narrative>
   </description>
