  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.5. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement labour market programmes and employment services for transitions to decent work over the life course, with particular focus on young and older workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The institutional capacity of the Public Employment Service and the Uzbekistan Public Work Programme were strengthened to deliver more effective responses to the COVID-19 crisis, both in the short-term and on the transition to sustainable economic and job recovery. This led to enhanced coordination between employment and social protection policies to improve the coverage and outreach of women and youth, and improved managerial skills, institutional arrangements and procedures required for a more effective crisis response.

The MELR implemented a series of recommendations as result of two institutional assessment carried out during 2020. As a consequence, the Public Work Programme, by October 2020, provided temporary work to approximately 500,000 people across Uzbekistan, of whom 270,000 were women, increasing by twice the capacity to deliver public jobs as compared to January 2020.  The institutional upgrading included improving the business process for rapid crisis response, strengthening monitoring mechanism, increased coordination and exchange between different databases concerning current and potential customers; improving the procedure for concluding new employment contracts, amending existing employment contracts, as well as the mandatory registration of cases of their termination.
 
Complementary measures were taken concerning benefits, including a new Law on employment (No. 642 October 2020), stipulating that unemployment benefits shall be at the level of the minimum wage and stating that the minimum wage for citizens involved in paid public works should be no less twice the state minimum wage.

Concerning coordination between employment and social protection policies, a national pilot programme (&quot;social contract&quot;) was launched to better coordinate social assistance and employment support services for vulnerable groups attended by the employment centres. In response to MELR&apos;s request for technical assistance, the ILO carried out two institutional assessments, one of the Public Employment Services and another of the Public Work Programme. The reports, diagnosis and policy recommendations were discussed with high level officials and working groups chaired by the First Deputy Minister. Follow up to the implementation was further provided in relation to services to vulnerable groups and targeted programmes for young people.

The ILO provided ad-hoc advice on how to adapt the employment services within the context of the COVID-19 crisis, while maintaining strategic objectives for sustainable employment recovery. The ILO supported an enhanced coordination between employment and social protection programmes, assisting on the design of the &quot;social contract&quot; for PES customers, identifying areas for an integrated management of the databases developed by different services, and revising the protocol for referrals among services.

The ILO provided technical comments to the first draft of the national programme for establishing Youth Employment and Career Guidance Centers, and organized a series of three webinars discussing international standards and best practices in this area. Country to country cooperation was facilitated with Germany and Denmark. In addition, the ILO prepared a report with an international overview on different country approaches for career guidance services for youth, and presented different models to be considered in the implementation of the national programme.</narrative>
   </description>
